        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">The Questions We'd Ask If We Were You.</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Three things families hesitate over. All three are deliberate. Here is the reasoning behind each one, in full.</p>
      </div>

      {/* Objection 1 */}
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-center mb-16">
        <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white shadow-sm">
          <img src="/showcases/ET_PrintSet.webp" alt="The three printed Sprouts books, the Teacher's Guide, Student Notebook and Read-Aloud Storybook, on a linen cloth." loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024" class="w-full h-auto" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mb-4" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">$249 is one purchase instead of six.</h3>
          <div class="font-body text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground space-y-3">
            <p>Here is the comparison we would want, so here it is. A typical year assembled from separate curricula means a science program, a nature study, a Bible curriculum, a handwriting or copywork book, an art component, and a read-aloud list. Six purchases, six shipping charges, six teacher's guides written by six people who never spoke to each other, and a stack of evenings spent making them line up.</p>
            <p>Eden's Table is one set. Thirty-six weeks, 180 daily lessons, and three books that were built together, week by week, as one year. Bible, science, language arts, math, art, history, geography, Latin, health, and character are already woven in, already scheduled, already telling you which day carries which.</p>
            <p>That is $249 for the year. Divided across 36 weeks, it is under $7 a week for your entire core. And it is one decision, made once, instead of six decisions you are still second-guessing in October.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-5 rounded-lg p-5" style="background-color:hsl(var(--eden-cream));border:1px solid hsl(var(--eden-gold) / 0.4)">
            <p class="font-body text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">What you are not also buying: a separate science curriculum. A separate nature study. A separate Bible curriculum. A separate art plan. A separate read-aloud list. A second evening of your week spent making all of them agree with each other.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Objection 2 */}
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-center mb-16">
        <div class="md:order-2 rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white shadow-sm border" style="border-color:hsl(var(--eden-gold) / 0.2)">
          {/* Was an Around the Table Faith card until 2026-09-12. The card decks
              came off the site with the kit, so the faith content is shown from
              the Teacher's Guide instead, which is a book a family can buy today. */}
          <img src="/showcases/Sprouts_W2_TG_Chamomile_p2_Monday.png" width="240" height="336" alt="A Monday lesson page from the Sprouts Teacher's Guide, Week 2, chamomile." loading="lazy" class="w-full h-auto" />
        </div>
        <div class="md:order-1">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mb-4" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">We worship the Creator. We study what He made.</h3>
          <div class="font-body text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground space-y-3">
            <p>This is the question we most want asked, so let us answer it completely.</p>
            <p>We believe the Creator God made everything. We believe we were placed, on purpose, in a garden, among plants and among people, and that both were made with complementary patterns. A plant that soothes and a body that can be soothed were designed to meet. That is not a coincidence and it is not magic. It is design.</p>
            <p>So when your child learns those patterns, she is doing what a person does in a museum built by someone she loves. She notices the craftsmanship, and the noticing sends her back to the One who did the work. Appreciation moves toward the Creator. It never stops at the creation.</p>
            <p>That line is not a marketing sentence for us. It is the line we edit against. No plant in these pages carries a life force of its own, and no created thing is given a spiritual property. Life has one source, and we name Him: Yahweh. Every energetic and every observation in this curriculum is drawn from historical Western herbal sources and taught as what it is: observed pattern in a made world. Herbalism here is stewardship of Yahweh's creation. It is not alternative medicine and it is not pantheism, and if we ever blur that, write to us and we will fix it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-5 rounded-lg p-5" style="background-color:hsl(var(--eden-cream));border:1px solid hsl(var(--eden-gold) / 0.4)">
            <p class="font-body text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">The test we run on every single line before it ships: Does this credit the plant, or does it credit the One who made the plant? If it credits the plant, it does not go in the book.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Objection 3 */}
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start mb-10">
        <div>
          <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white shadow-sm" style="max-width:420px">
            <img src="/showcases/ET_Founder_2026.jpg" alt="Camila, founder of The Eden Institute, smiling beneath the painted canopy of a carousel." loading="lazy" width="643" height="717" class="w-full h-auto" />
          </div>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-muted-foreground mt-2" style="max-width:420px">Camila. Master's in Education, credentialed classroom teacher, and the person who answers your email.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mb-4" style="color:hsl(var(--eden-bark))">We only move at the speed of cash.</h3>
          <div class="font-body text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground space-y-3">
            <p>No debt, no investors, no line of credit. This company does what we teach families to do, which is live inside what it actually has. The cost of that conviction is time, and we are not going to pretend otherwise or hide it behind a cheerful shipping date.</p>
            <p>That is why the shelf here is short. The three printed books are finished and they print to order, one set at a time, as families ask for them. There is no warehouse, no back order and no minimum we had to hit before your copy could exist.</p>
            <p>So the honest version is this. You are buying a finished thing at a fair price, and that is what builds the next one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

